5. Critical Decision and Design Evaluation
Critical decision: Whether to select Job A with energy savings of 100 or reject it in favour of Jobs B, C, D and E with combined savings of 160.

Does the algorithm produce the optimal result? Yes. It selects B, C, D, E and F, achieving 180.

Does any decision rule fail? No. The include/exclude recurrence correctly compares the complete compatible solutions instead of relying on individual job values.

6. Conclusion
The adversarial dataset demonstrates that selecting the job with the highest individual energy-saving value may lead to a poor overall result. However, the Dynamic Programming algorithm successfully handles this situation by comparing inclusion and exclusion values at every state. It produces the optimal total energy-saving value of 180.

The test confirms that the algorithm is more reliable than a simple greedy selection strategy for weighted job scheduling
